        "## Frequency thresholding and vocabulary selection\n",
        "\n",
        "In real corpora, the vocabulary can be very large.  A common method is to keep only tokens whose frequency is at least a threshold $r$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "V_r = \\{v: f(v)\\ge r\\},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $f(v)$ is the corpus frequency of token $v$.\n",
        "\n",
        "This creates a smaller vocabulary but discards rare tokens.  Rare tokens may be noise, but they may also be important names, mathematical terms, or technical concepts.\n",
        "\n",
        "The threshold $r$ therefore controls a bias-variance-like tradeoff:\n",
        "\n",
        "- large vocabulary: more expressive, but sparse and harder to estimate;\n",
        "- small vocabulary: more stable, but less informative.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Vocabulary selection as estimation.**  If $p(v)$ is the unknown probability of token $v$ and $\\hat p(v)=f(v)/M$ is its empirical frequency from $M$ observed tokens, then low-frequency tokens have high relative uncertainty.  Removing rare tokens is a form of regularization.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
