        "The mathematical point of the explorer is that the dimension $d$ of the vector space $\\mathbb{R}^d$ is not intrinsic to the corpus.  It is chosen by the vocabulary construction rule.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Unknown tokens\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose a word tokenizer is trained on a corpus whose vocabulary is $V$.  When a new document contains a token not in $V$, one classical solution is to use a special token `[UNK]`.\n",
        "\n",
        "Mathematically, extend the vocabulary to\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "V' = V\\cup \\{\\text{[UNK]}\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Then define\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\pi(v)=\n",
        "\\begin{cases}\n",
        "v, & v\\in V,\\\\\n",
        "\\text{[UNK]}, & v\\notin V.\n",
        "\\end{cases}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This projection collapses all unseen tokens into a single symbol.  The price is severe information loss.  For example, all of the following may become `[UNK]`:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "ChatGPT\n",
        "Noetherian\n",
        "eigenvector\n",
        "Boston\n",
        "mispeledword\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The unknown token solves the finite-vocabulary problem but destroys distinctions among rare and unseen terms.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Subword tokenization as a remedy\n",
        "\n",
        "Subword tokenization reduces the unknown-token problem by building words from smaller units.  If the vocabulary contains common pieces such as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{eigen}, \\quad \\text{vector}, \\quad \\text{ization}, \\quad \\text{pre}, \\quad \\text{train},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "then rare words may be represented as sequences of meaningful or semi-meaningful pieces.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{eigenvectorization}\n",
        "\\mapsto\n",
        "(\\text{eigen},\\text{vector},\\text{ization}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The word may not be in the vocabulary, but its parts may be.\n",
        "\n",
        "## A clean view of byte-pair encoding\n",
        "\n",
        "Byte-pair encoding, often abbreviated BPE, is a common subword vocabulary construction method.  The exact implementations used in production systems vary, but the core mathematical idea is simple.\n",
        "\n",
        "Start with a corpus represented as sequences of basic symbols, often characters or bytes.  Repeatedly merge the most frequent adjacent pair into a new token.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Abstract BPE algorithm\n",
        "\n",
        "Let the current tokenized corpus be a finite multiset of sequences over a vocabulary $V_k$.  At step $k$:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. count all adjacent token pairs $(a,b)$ in the corpus;\n",
        "2. find a most frequent pair\n",
        "\n",
        "   $$\n",
        "   (a_k,b_k)\\in \\arg\\max_{(a,b)} f_k(a,b);\n",
        "   $$\n",
        "\n",
        "3. create a new token\n",
        "\n",
        "   $$\n",
        "   z_k = a_k b_k;\n",
        "   $$\n",
        "\n",
        "4. replace occurrences of $(a_k,b_k)$ by $z_k$;\n",
        "5. update the vocabulary\n",
        "\n",
        "   $$\n",
        "   V_{k+1}=V_k\\cup\\{z_k\\}.\n",
        "   $$\n",
        "\n",
        "After $K$ merges, the vocabulary has grown from basic symbols to useful subwords.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**BPE as greedy compression.**  BPE greedily chooses frequent adjacent pairs.  It is not optimizing a simple global convex objective, but it can be interpreted as building a compact code for frequent local patterns.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Python example: a tiny BPE demonstration\n",
        "\n",
        "The following code implements a small educational version of BPE.  It is not intended to match production tokenizers.  Its purpose is to reveal the mathematical structure."
