        "\n",
        "## Tokenization and probability spaces\n",
        "\n",
        "Tokenization changes the probability space of language modeling.  If tokens are words, then a language model estimates probabilities such as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(W_t=\\text{models}\\mid W_1,\\ldots,W_{t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If tokens are subwords, then the random variables are subword tokens:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(T_t=\\text{mod}\\mid T_1,\\ldots,T_{t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "These are not the same statistical problem.  The vocabulary determines the categorical sample space.\n",
        "\n",
        "For a vocabulary of size $d$, a next-token model outputs a probability vector\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_t=(p_{t1},\\ldots,p_{td})\\in \\Delta^{d-1},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\Delta^{d-1}=\\left\\{p\\in\\mathbb{R}^d: p_j\\ge 0,\\ \\sum_{j=1}^d p_j=1\\right\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus changing the tokenizer changes $d$, the dimension of the output simplex.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Empirical token distribution\n",
        "\n",
        "Given a tokenized corpus with total number of tokens $M$, define the empirical token distribution\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat p_j = \\frac{f_j}{M},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $f_j$ is the frequency of token $v_j$.\n",
        "\n",
        "The vector\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat p=(\\hat p_1,\\ldots,\\hat p_d)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "belongs to the probability simplex $\\Delta^{d-1}$.  It is a simple but important statistical summary of a corpus.\n",
        "\n",
        "The empirical entropy is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat H = -\\sum_{j=1}^d \\hat p_j \\log \\hat p_j,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where terms with $\\hat p_j=0$ are omitted.  A corpus whose token frequencies are evenly spread has larger entropy than one dominated by a few common tokens."
