        "## Zipf-like behavior\n",
        "\n",
        "In large natural language corpora, token frequencies tend to be highly skewed.  A small number of tokens occur very frequently, while many tokens occur rarely.  This qualitative pattern is often called Zipf-like behavior.\n",
        "\n",
        "If tokens are ranked by decreasing frequency, the frequency of the token with rank $r$ often roughly follows a power-law shape:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f_r \\approx C r^{-\\alpha}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "for constants $C>0$ and $\\alpha>0$ over a range of ranks.\n",
        "\n",
        "This matters because rare tokens are not an exception.  They are a structural feature of language data.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Vocabulary growth and Heaps-like behavior\n",
        "\n",
        "Another common empirical pattern is that the number of distinct observed tokens grows as more text is collected.  If $M$ is the number of observed tokens and $V(M)$ is the number of distinct token types, then one often sees approximate behavior of the form\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "V(M) \\approx K M^\\beta,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $0<\\beta<1$.\n",
        "\n",
        "The implication is important: collecting more data keeps revealing new tokens.  Therefore a fixed word vocabulary will always face rare and unseen words.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Sequence length versus vocabulary size\n",
        "\n",
        "Tokenization involves a fundamental tradeoff:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{small vocabulary} \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow \\quad \\text{longer sequences},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{large vocabulary} \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow \\quad \\text{shorter sequences}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Character tokenization has a very small vocabulary but long sequences.  Word tokenization has shorter sequences but a huge vocabulary.  Subword tokenization lives between these extremes.\n",
        "\n",
        "This matters computationally.  In a transformer, self-attention has a cost that grows roughly quadratically with sequence length $m$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{attention cost} \\propto m^2.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A tokenizer that produces longer sequences can substantially increase computational cost.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Padding and truncation\n",
        "\n",
        "Neural network batches often require sequences of equal length.  If a batch contains token sequences\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(t_{i1},\\ldots,t_{im_i}), \\qquad i=1,\\ldots,B,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "then a maximum length $L$ is chosen.  Shorter sequences are padded using a special token `[PAD]`, and longer sequences are truncated.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(7,3,8) \\mapsto (7,3,8,0,0)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "if `[PAD]` has index $0$ and $L=5$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Padding is another representation choice.  Models must be told which positions are real and which are padding.  This is done using an attention mask or sequence mask.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Special tokens\n",
        "\n",
        "Many NLP systems add special tokens to the vocabulary.  Common examples include:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Token | Meaning |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| `[PAD]` | padding token |\n",
        "| `[UNK]` | unknown token |\n",
        "| `[BOS]` | beginning of sequence |\n",
        "| `[EOS]` | end of sequence |\n",
        "| `[MASK]` | masked token for prediction tasks |\n",
        "| `[CLS]` | classification token in some encoder models |\n",
        "| `[SEP]` | separator token between segments |\n",
        "\n",
        "Mathematically, special tokens are ordinary vocabulary elements with assigned indices.  Their meaning comes from how the model and loss function use them.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Tokenization for AI systems\n",
        "\n",
        "Tokenization is especially important in modern AI systems because language models operate on token sequences, not directly on words.  A prompt, a retrieved document, or a generated answer is represented internally as tokens.\n",
        "\n",
        "This affects several practical AI questions.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Context length\n",
        "\n",
        "If a model has a maximum context length $L$, then the tokenized input must satisfy\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "m \\le L.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Here $m$ is the number of tokens after tokenization, not the number of words.  A text that looks short to a human may be long in tokens if it contains code, formulas, tables, or uncommon terms.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Retrieval-augmented generation\n",
        "\n",
        "In retrieval-augmented generation, documents are often split into chunks before being embedded and retrieved.  A chunking rule is partly a tokenization problem.  It determines the units that become searchable objects.\n",
        "\n",
        "A simple chunking map has the form\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\gamma:A^*\\to (A^*)^q,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where a document is divided into $q$ text chunks.  Each chunk is then tokenized and embedded.\n",
        "\n",
        "Bad chunking may separate definitions from examples, theorem statements from assumptions, or student questions from answers.  Good chunking respects mathematical and semantic structure.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Prompt design\n",
        "\n",
        "A prompt is also a token sequence.  Repetition, formatting, examples, and equations all affect the tokenized representation.  Therefore prompt design has a mathematical side: it changes the conditioning information available to a next-token probability model.\n",
        "\n",
